## Proof

We list the 7 boats and count only **rowers** (not coxswains), since the question asks "How many **rowers** will start?" and warns us to "Pay close attention to all designations!"

| # | Boat | Rowers per boat | Number of such boats | Rowers |
|---|------|----------------|---------------------|--------|
| 1 | Single scull | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| 2 | Coxless double scull | 2 | 2 | 4 |
| 3 | Double scull **with coxswain** | 2 (the third person is the **coxswain**, not a rower) | 1 | 2 |
| 4 | Coxless four | 4 | 1 | 4 |
| 5 | Coxed four | 4 (the fifth person is the coxswain) | 1 | 4 |
| 6 | Coxed eight | 8 (the ninth person is the coxswain) | 1 | 8 |

**Key point — the trap.** The note states "In the double scull with coxswain, there are three rowers." This is deliberately misleading: a double scull with coxswain has **2 rowers + 1 coxswain = 3 people**, but only 2 of them are rowers. The designation "with coxswain" explicitly marks the third person as a coxswain, who does **not** row. The same applies to the coxed four (4 rowers + 1 coxswain) and the coxed eight (8 rowers + 1 coxswain). The instruction "Pay close attention to all designations!" is precisely warning us not to count coxswains as rowers.

**Total rowers:**

$$1 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 4 + 8 = \boxed{23}$$

